Inside one tenant's database
On the left, the vault: names, national identifiers and contact details, each
sealed with a key belonging to that one person. On the right, everything else -
records, their history, the runs and the audit trail - where a person appears
as a pseudonym and nothing else. Below, what each party can read: a participant
sees what its claimed run named and nothing behind it; the operator sees that
work ran, how long it took and one word for how it ended, with no name and no
payload; an auditor of the tenant sees who acted, on what, when and under which
run, as a pseudonym until the vault is consulted. Turning a pseudonym back into
a person is a recorded read of the vault.
